  "detail": "Prompt busy replies now exhaust the wait budget and exit 75 without running the binary, which fixes that part of the earlier finding. Slow replies still fail open, though. `wrapperLeaseClient` falls back to the Unix socket on any HTTP error, including a timeout, and `withGateLease` (src/cli/gate-slot.ts:152) then treats the socket's error as proof that the service is unreachable, because it isn't a `DaemonTimeoutError`. Reproduced with a temporary probe:\n- An HTTP service that answers after 1.3 s and an absent socket: the real binary started unslotted after 1.07 s of a 5 s budget. The same service then returned a valid busy reply.\n- 13 busy replies, each delayed past the 2 s request timeout: the binary ran unslotted at 31.9 s of a 60 s budget, because `lastReply` only advances on a reply that arrives in time.\n\nThe daemon is slowest exactly when the machine is loaded, which is when slotting matters. Fix: keep a timeout's classification through the transport fallback, so a timeout followed by a socket error still counts as a timeout. Count a late reply as proof the service is alive. Run unslotted only when every transport failed to connect, never because a request was slow. Test: with a fake HTTP client that times out and a socket client that throws a connection error, and with replies delayed past the request timeout, assert the binary doesn't start and the wrapper exits 75 at its budget."
